### Dynamic Array Creation in C

In C, dynamic arrays are created using memory allocation functions from `<stdlib.h>`, such as `malloc()` or `calloc()`. These functions allocate memory from the heap during runtime rather than the stack during compile time.

#### Syntax/Example Code:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int n;
    printf("Enter size of array: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    // Allocating memory dynamically
    int *arr = (int *)malloc(n * sizeof(int));
    if (arr == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed!\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Initialize and print
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        arr[i] = i + 1;
    }

    // Free allocated memory
    free(arr);
    return 0;
}

```

#### Performance Improvements:
1. **Optimal Memory Utilization**: Prevents wasted memory (under-allocation) or stack overflow (over-allocation) because the exact size is determined at runtime.
2. **Avoidance of Stack Overflow**: Large datasets can exceed the limited stack memory (which causes crashes). Heap memory is significantly larger, allowing programs to handle much bigger workloads.
3. **Flexibility and Resizing**: Dynamic arrays can be resized using `realloc()` without restarting the application or copying overhead manually, maintaining continuous data structures efficiently.

### C Program using Structures and Pointers

Using pointers with structures improves efficiency dramatically by avoiding the copying of large structure data when passing them to functions.

#### Code implementation:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Student {
    char name[50];
    int roll_no;
    float marks;
};

// Passing struct pointer to function avoids copying overhead
void displayStudent(const struct Student *s) {
    printf("\nStudent Information:\n");
    printf("Name: %s\n", s->name);
    printf("Roll No: %d\n", s->roll_no);
    printf("Marks: %.2f\n", s->marks);
}

int main() {
    struct Student s1;
    struct Student *ptr = &s1;

    printf("Enter Name: ");
    scanf("%s", ptr->name);
    printf("Enter Roll No: ");
    scanf("%d", &ptr->roll_no);
    printf("Enter Marks: ");
    scanf("%f", &ptr->marks);

    displayStudent(ptr);
    return 0;
}

```

#### Why Pointers Improve Efficiency:
1. **Pass-by-Reference**: Passing a pointer to a struct takes only 4 or 8 bytes (address size) instead of copying the entire structure (which can be hundreds of bytes), saving both CPU cycles and stack space.
2. **Dynamic Modification**: Functions can modify the actual student structure directly without returning a copy.

### AVL Tree Rotations and Notations

AVL trees maintain balance by ensuring the heights of two child subtrees of any node differ by at most one ($BF \in \{-1, 0, 1\}$). If an insertion or deletion causes $|BF| > 1$, we apply one of four rotation routines:

#### 1. Single Left Rotation (LL Rotation / RR Imbalance)
Applied when a node becomes unbalanced due to insertion in the right subtree of its right child.
- **Example**: `10 -> 20 -> 30`.
- **Routine**:

```c
Node* rotateLeft(Node* k2) {
    Node* k1 = k2->right;
    k2->right = k1->left;
    k1->left = k2;
    updateHeight(k2);
    updateHeight(k1);
    return k1;
}

```

#### 2. Single Right Rotation (RR Rotation / LL Imbalance)
Applied when insertion happens in the left subtree of the left child.
- **Example**: `30 -> 20 -> 10`.
- **Routine**:

```c
Node* rotateRight(Node* k2) {
    Node* k1 = k2->left;
    k2->left = k1->right;
    k1->right = k2;
    updateHeight(k2);
    updateHeight(k1);
    return k1;
}

```

#### 3. Double Left-Right Rotation (LR Rotation)
Applied when insertion is in the right subtree of the left child. It is performed by a left rotation on the left child, followed by a right rotation on the parent.
- **Routine**:

```c
Node* rotateLeftRight(Node* k3) {
    k3->left = rotateLeft(k3->left);
    return rotateRight(k3);
}

```

#### 4. Double Right-Left Rotation (RL Rotation)
Applied when insertion is in the left subtree of the right child.
- **Routine**:

```c
Node* rotateRightLeft(Node* k3) {
    k3->right = rotateRight(k3->right);
    return rotateLeft(k3);
}
```
